WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
The chain rule is a sum over paths.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:08.429
The single most important detail in this lecture is what happens when a value is used more than once.

3
00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:12.421
Suppose L depends on a through two intermediate nodes, c and d.

4
00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:26.035
The multivariable chain rule says the partial of L with respect to a is the sum: (partial L over partial c)(partial c over partial a) plus (partial L over partial d)(partial d over partial a).

5
00:00:26.315 --> 00:00:36.811
In general, the derivative of the output with respect to any node is the sum, over every path from that node to the output, of the product of local derivatives along the path.

6
00:00:37.091 --> 00:00:39.651
In code, that sum becomes accumulation.

7
00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:45.499
Each node’s gradient starts at zero, and every consumer adds its contribution: self.

8
00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.016
grad +=.

9
00:00:47.296 --> 00:00:48.277
, never self.

10
00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:49.581
grad =.

11
00:00:49.861 --> 00:00:59.355
The video discovers the overwrite version at 01:22:28 with b = a + a, whose gradient should be 2 and comes out as 1.

12
00:00:59.635 --> 00:01:01.128
Two consequences follow.

13
00:01:01.408 --> 00:01:06.507
First, a node can only pass gradient on once every consumer has added its share.

14
00:01:06.787 --> 00:01:13.869
That’s why the backward pass visits nodes in reverse topological order (every node after all nodes that depend on it).

15
00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:21.360
Second, gradients must be reset to zero before each new backward pass, or the next pass adds on top of the old one.

16
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.669
You’ll meet that second bug in the training loop next lesson.
